{"candidates":[{"content":"Add a warrant-priority layer instead of any graph enrichment: rank each attack edge by the proof-depth (length of the shortest undefeated defense chain) of its source's warrant, and compute the grounded labeling by iterating defeat resolution in ascending (priority, node-id) order, freezing each decision once made. Determinism is preserved because the iteration order is canonical; cost stays polynomial because defense chains are computed once and cached. The criticizable claim is that this breaks the neighbourhood's covering principle (SRC_007): the priority ordering is a property of the iteration, not of a monotone edge-construction pass, so it is not expressible as an admissible enrichment at the edge layer and its fixed point may differ from any edge-enriched grounded extension.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"73c33e65d29d6ed0e48bbb9ae73e4f604fa2682b6f48690875e28e06210dc660"},{"role":"dependence","target":"86232ebf5626efc5d29fb5630b9284af34a93dae63929c770bc111d0c4e4e61a"},{"role":"dependence","target":"e093eb8611ff0d96fc197357178adfbf4e9e7c0ba8471f3c76aa9f21a3aea46a"}],"typicality":0.22},{"content":"On each refutation, rewrite the graph structurally: replace the refuting subgraph with a single canonical 'defeater' node whose outgoing attacks are the union of the refuting subgraph's attacks, and define the grounded extension over the fully normalized graph. Unlike RESIDUE (SRC_005), which annotates the final labeling, this carries the reason a node stays OUT in the graph shape itself, so reinstatement still emerges from the fixed point over the normalized graph. Criticizable points: confluence of the rewrite under arbitrary refutation orderings is unproven, and the rewrite changes the graph that later edge-construction passes (SRC_002) see, so interaction with fresh-warrant re-examination (SRC_006) is underspecified.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"5431feda66e41c92d61607a482c76cb05bd782c10644d596f9f903e82e4e8c2f"},{"role":"dependence","target":"f5a262c5a6bd74029c9528d1063e7a6b5d96c4ee2e4753c79d6fed6817d3fa9b"},{"role":"dependence","target":"e093eb8611ff0d96fc197357178adfbf4e9e7c0ba8471f3c76aa9f21a3aea46a"}],"typicality":0.3},{"content":"Compute the grounded extension lazily for a target conjecture: expand only the subgraph reachable by attack and defense edges up to the first fixpoint hit, memoize labels of visited nodes, and certify the result by a polynomial check that no omitted node can reach the target through an undefeated attacker path. This changes the cost profile (potentially sub-linear in practice) while keeping worst-case polynomial, and moves the determinism guarantee from the full-graph fixed point to a fixed traversal order. The criticizable claim is that the certification check is itself a fixed-point computation in disguise, so the 'lazy' version may merely re-derive the full extension in the worst case and add bookkeeping without a semantic strengthening.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"73c33e65d29d6ed0e48bbb9ae73e4f604fa2682b6f48690875e28e06210dc660"},{"role":"dependence","target":"f5a262c5a6bd74029c9528d1063e7a6b5d96c4ee2e4753c79d6fed6817d3fa9b"}],"typicality":0.35},{"content":"Strengthen the validity of committed roots by adding an irredundancy requirement: a root conjecture is committed only if deleting it and its incident attacks changes the grounded labeling of at least one other node; verify this with one additional fixed-point pass per root candidate. This is a property of the root set, not of the edge-construction layer, so it composes with any admissible enrichment (SRC_002, SRC_007) without altering the fixed-point operator. Criticizable points: it multiplies cost by the number of roots (breaking the spirit of the single-pass polynomial guarantee), and it presupposes a canonical definition of 'changes the labeling' that may itself depend on which enrichment was applied.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"e093eb8611ff0d96fc197357178adfbf4e9e7c0ba8471f3c76aa9f21a3aea46a"},{"role":"dependence","target":"86232ebf5626efc5d29fb5630b9284af34a93dae63929c770bc111d0c4e4e61a"},{"role":"dependence","target":"30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"}],"typicality":0.42},{"content":"Split the fixed-point computation into two passes: Pass 1 computes the skeptical labeling under a restricted rule that disallows reinstatement (a node is accepted only if directly defended by an attacked attacker); Pass 2 promotes to the full extension only if the restricted labeling's accepted set equals the intersection of all complete extensions restricted to that subgraph, i.e., reinstatement is admitted only when it introduces no new conflict. This makes reinstatement a checked, gated step rather than a purely derived one, changing the accepted/rejected boundary for cyclic cases. Determinism is preserved because both passes are deterministic; the criticizable claim is that the Pass-2 gate is not polynomial in general, and that 'no new conflict' smuggles in a second semantic commitment that may not respect the school's unifier stance (SRC_001).","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"73c33e65d29d6ed0e48bbb9ae73e4f604fa2682b6f48690875e28e06210dc660"},{"role":"dependence","target":"30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"}],"typicality":0.28},{"content":"Make the school a first-class input: parameterize the grounded extension by an explicit selector that, among a finite family of monotone fixed-point operators, picks the one whose accepted set is the maximal sub-relation satisfying the school's stance (unifier vs. skeptic), computed by a single monotone pass over the operator lattice. Determinism is preserved by committing the school as part of the input; validity of committed roots is then checked per school. The criticizable claim is that the selection rule over schools is itself non-monotone and may be non-polynomial, and that 'maximal sub-relation satisfying a stance' is not well-defined without a canonical ordering over relations — so the proposal may displace the hardness rather than removing it.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"},{"role":"dependence","target":"10d18e6652e2cc898265363af979941f9a08d15f3c48e6b767a83bdc505adf2b"}],"typicality":0.18}],"simulation_proposals":[]}